Reactive Arthritis
Complex Fatigue Entities
Sterile joint inflammation post-GI/GU infection

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-045
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of acute asymmetrical joint pain, eye redness, and severe painful urination to rule out acute gonococcal arthritis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden onset of excruciating pain and swelling in the right ankle, accompanied by severe dysuria and bilateral red eyes. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to differentiate between acute disseminated bacterial infection requiring emergent IV antibiotics and an acute, sterile autoimmune flare, prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My ankle is totally swollen and killing me, it burns like fire when I pee, and my eyes are completely bloodshot.
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a rapid 48-hour onset of classic reactive symptomology. The patient reports a documented episode of severe infectious gastroenteritis (Campylobacter) approximately 3 weeks ago. The current presentation includes an acute, painful oligoarthritis primarily affecting the right ankle, bilateral conjunctivitis (gritty, red eyes without thick purulent discharge), and severe urethritis (burning dysuria). The patient also notes profound systemic fatigue and malaise over the last 2 days.
Functional Status: Ambulatory with a severe limp; using furniture to offload weight from the right ankle.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to stand for prolonged periods safely due to joint pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 37.5 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, guarding the right lower limb, frequent blinking due to ocular irritation. Low-grade fever present.
Physical Exam: Right ankle demonstrates moderate, warm, asymmetric effusion with marked tenderness over the Achilles tendon insertion (enthesitis). Left knee and upper limbs are entirely normal. Bilateral conjunctivae are deeply injected but lack purulent exudate; corneas appear clear. Abdomen is soft and non-tender. Urinalysis reveals trace leukocytes but negative nitrites. Clinical triad strongly confirms sterile joint inflammation post-GI infection.
Environmental Observations: Clean, safe environment. No obvious trip hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent antibiotics. OTC Ibuprofen provides minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Reactive Arthritis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Disseminated Gonococcal Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear chronological link to a recent GI infection rather than a new STI, absence of characteristic necrotic skin pustules, and conjunctivitis which is a classic non-infectious feature of the reactive triad.

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Multi-system involvement (eyes, urethra, entheses) which strongly points to a systemic immune-mediated process rather than isolated bacterial joint seeding, and lack of high-grade fever.

Gout - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Simultaneous presence of prominent extra-articular features (conjunctivitis, urethritis) and enthesitis at the Achilles, which are not typical for a standard crystal arthropathy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of a swollen joint accompanied by systemic signs (fever, dysuria, eye redness) mandated immediate, in-person assessment to clinically exclude severe, joint-destroying bacterial infections such as acute septic arthritis or disseminated gonococcal infection. Having confirmed the classic extra-articular triad consistent with a post-infectious, sterile autoimmune reaction, urgent initiation of potent systemic anti-inflammatories was required to halt the synovial inflammation, manage the extreme discomfort, and prevent a highly traumatic and unnecessary emergency department admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg for rapid systemic suppression of synovial and entheseal inflammation. Prescribed an oral course of Meloxicam 15mg daily. Targeted intervention strategy includes Low Dose Doxycycline and COX-2 inhibitors. Advised use of over-the-counter lubricating eye drops.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your anti-inflammatory medication strictly with a full breakfast every morning, you will establish a consistent, protective level of medication in your bloodstream, directly suppressing the joint swelling and rewarding yourself with improved, pain-free mobility throughout the day.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: None required.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 3 days for comprehensive STI screening (to absolutely exclude concurrent infection) and formal rheumatological blood panels.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if the ankle joint becomes increasingly fiery red, extremely hot, and tense, if vision suddenly blurs or drops, or if high fever and severe shivering develop.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Reactive arthritis is a clinical diagnosis based on pattern recognition; laboratory testing is necessary to officially rule out underlying active infections.
Clinical Course: The acute joint swelling and eye redness will gradually subside over the next 1-2 weeks with continuous anti-inflammatory therapy. The urethritis usually resolves independently but requires screening.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you develop uncontrollable shivering/fever, if your ankle suddenly becomes fiery red and agonizingly hot, or if your vision suddenly blurs.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is confused by your recent stomach infection and is mistakenly attacking your own healthy joints, eyes, and urethra; the strong anti-inflammatory medication acts to turn off this false immune attack.
Actionable Steps: Use preservative-free artificial tears every 2 hours tomorrow; aggressively lubricating the eye surface directly flushes out inflammatory chemicals, positively reinforcing a faster reduction in redness and gritty discomfort.

